  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nEnsemble members are in good agreement that by Tuesday drier air \nwill start to filter back into the region as the subtropical high \nrestrengthens and starts to retrograde westward back to over our \nregion. By the middle of the week the center of the high will be \nstraddling the AZ/NM border and by the end of the week the center \nof the high is forecast to be over AZ. As the high moves \noverhead, PWATs are shown to drop into a 0.9-1.2\" range across \nour region and with warmer temperatures aloft and subsidence\nchances for thunderstorms will be poor across the region. The NBM\nshows that Tuesday and Wednesday there could be enough moisture \naround to support isolated thunderstorm chances for the higher \nterrain areas. But by the end of next week thunderstorm chances \nfor our area shut off and will mainly be relegated to the Mogollon\nRim. \n\nUnusually hot temperatures will be the main story next week. As \nmentioned above the subtropical high will slowly retrograde back \nover our region beginning on Tuesday, which will result in a quick \nwarming trend for our area. The high is also expected to \nstrengthen to around 596-598dam during this time. The combination \nof the center settling over our region and strengthening will \nresult in temperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) are \nforecasted to be in the 112-118 (85-93) degree range across the \nlower deserts and in the 102-112 (75-85) degree range across the \nhigher terrain areas. There is a good chance daily record highs \nand warm lows will be tied or broken and temperatures may even\nnear monthly records. The increase in temperatures will also \nresult in an increase in coverage of Major HeatRisk, back to \nwidespread beginning Tuesday and then widespread Extreme HeatRisk \nby Thursday. Confidence in the heat forecast for next week remains\nhigh. So, the Extreme Heat Warning will continue through the \nworkweek and has now been extended through Saturday, in \ncollaboration with the Tucson office. The warning will likely be \nexpanded to our entire CWA by the middle of the week with the \ndevelopment of widespread Extreme HeatRisk.",
